{"type":"html","id":"ggjdaTYSzHf_alt_1978768214","label":"ggjdaTYSzHf_alt_1978768214","default":"Alt Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggjdaTYSzHf_title_-2124750075","label":"ggjdaTYSzHf_title_-2124750075","default":"Image Title"},{"type":"html","id":"ggCETw5zgB2_srcSet_desktop_src_-555133884","label":"ggCETw5zgB2_srcSet_desktop_src_-555133884","default":"https://ucarecdn.com/ee6d5074-1640-4cc7-8933-47c8589c3dee/-/format/auto/"},{"type":"html","id":"ggCETw5zgB2_alt_1978768214","label":"ggCETw5zgB2_alt_1978768214","default":"Alt Image"},{"type":"html","id":"ggCETw5zgB2_title_-2124750075","label":"ggCETw5zgB2_title_-2124750075","default":"Image Title"},{"type":"html","id":"ggJP2YAbWFp_text_-116692092","label":"ggJP2YAbWFp_text_-116692092","default":"<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:15px;\">Microchipping your pet is a crucial step to ensuring their safety and security. A&nbsp;</span><a href=\"https://avmajournals.avma.org/view/journals/javma/235/2/javma.235.2.160.xml\"><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#1155cc;font-size:15px;\"><u>study</u></span></a><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:15px;\"> of 7,700 stray animals at shelters revealed that microchipped dogs were returned to their owners 52.2% of the time, compared to only 21.9% for dogs without microchips. These statistics underscore the importance of microchipping in reuniting lost pets with their families.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:15px;\">But what exactly is a microchip? It's a tiny computer chip, no larger than a grain of rice, implanted under your pet's skin, typically between the shoulder blades. This procedure is similar to a routine vaccination, performed by a veterinarian. Once implanted, the microchip remains dormant until activated by a scanner. When scanned, the chip emits a unique identification number, which is then displayed on the scanner's screen. This number is linked to a database containing your contact information, making it easier for shelters and veterinary clinics to reunite you with your pet if they become lost.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:15px;\">One common question pet owners have is about the cost of microchipping. While the price may vary depending on your location, generally, it's an affordable procedure, usually costing less than $100. And the best part? Once implanted, the microchip lasts for the lifetime of your pet, never expiring and always ready to be scanned by any universal scanner.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:15px;\">The benefits of microchipping extend beyond just identification. It provides peace of mind, knowing that your pet has a permanent form of identification that cannot be lost or removed. Additionally, microchips are especially crucial for puppies, as they can be microchipped as early as 6 to 8 weeks of age, ensuring their protection from a young age.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#000000;font-size:15px;\">While microchipping is an essential step, it's important to remember that it should not replace other forms of identification, such as collars and tags. These additional measures act as a backup in case your pet is found by someone without scanning technology. By combining microchipping with collars and tags, you maximize the chances of a swift reunion with your beloved furry friend.</span></p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggRl8up_RnD_text_-1893723408","label":"ggRl8up_RnD_text_-1893723408","default":"Recent Blogs"},{"type":"html","id":"gglT0Qantet_srcSet_desktop_src_-555133884","label":"gglT0Qantet_srcSet_desktop_src_-555133884","default":"https://ucarecdn.com/ee6d5074-1640-4cc7-8933-47c8589c3dee/-/format/auto/"},{"type":"html","id":"gglT0Qantet_alt_1978768214","label":"gglT0Qantet_alt_1978768214","default":"Alt Image